* Deploying = how to put a React app on the Internet.

* Buying a physical computer for our backend is too mcuh of a HASSLE.
  - We'd have to keep it running for 24 hours somewhere.

  - Instead RENT a computer from AWS.

  - AWS manage running of computer for us.

* AWS (Amazon Web Services) = collection of services that help us put stuff on the Internet.
  - Biggest service for deploying websites on the Internet.

* EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) = lets us rent a computer from AWS.
  - AKA Infrastructure as a Service(IaaS)

  - Means AWS manages the infrastructure or computers for us.

  - 1 Instance = 1 EC2 computer

* To rent a computer on AWS:
  - Open EC2

  - Open Instances tab

  - Then click "Launch Instances"

* BUT EC2 only gives us a COMPUTER. We still need to:
  - Install NodeJS

  - Connect it to the Internet

  - Upload our code

* To solve SETUP on AWS, we can use the "Elastic Beanstalk" service.
  - Search "Elastic Beanstalk" in AWS.

  - Think of "Elastic Beanstalk" as layer on top of EC2.

* Elastic Beanstalk uses EC2 to rent a computer and installs all the software we need.
  - It also connects the computer to the Internet.

  - Elastic Beanstalk does all the software setup for us.

  - All we need to do, is just upload our code to Elastic Beanstalk. AKA Platform as a Service (PaaS).

* Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS).
   - This means it manages the infrastructure and software for us.

*  Create environment on Elastic Beanstalk page.
  - Click on "Environments" tab.

  - Click "Create Environment" button.

  - In "Configure Environment" page, select "Web server environment" for the "Environment Tier"

  - Provide an application name in "Application Information"

  - In "Platform" section, we select the technology we want to use.
    - For "Platform" dropdown, select "Node.js"

  - For "Application code", we can:
    - Use the "Sample Application"

    - Or uplaod our own code.

  - For "Presets", choose:
    - "Single instance (free tier eligible)"
  

* Role = gives Elastic Beanstalk permission to use other services in AWS.
  - We need to create one.

* For the "Configure service access":
  - In "Service Role" section, click "Create role" and use the default values.
    - Trusted entity type:
      - "AWS service"
    
    - Use case:
      - "Elastic Beanstalk - Environment"

  - Refresh "Service role" and select role we just created.

  - In "EC2 instance profile", we also need to click "Create role". Use the same default values for creation.

    - Refresh "EC2 instance profile" and select role we just created.

  - Can leave "EC2 key pair - optional" blank.


* For "Set up networking, database, and tags - optional", just use default values and click "next".

* For "Configure instance traffic and scaling - optional", scroll down to "Capacity".
  - In "Capacity", got to dropdowns in "Instance Types".
  
  - Here, we get to choose the size of the computer we want to rent from EC2.

  - If you created your account after July 15, 2025 use these instance types:
    - t3.micro
    - t3.small

* For "Configure updates, monitoring, and logging - optional", use the default options.

* Finally click "Create".
  - Elastic Benastalk is now going to:

    - Rent a computer using EC2.

    - Install needed software onto computer like NodeJS and npm.

    - Do all the configs to connect computer to the Internet.

* We can now select our domain to open the website.

  - http://ecommerce-project-react-env.eba-d3sphc7z.us-east-2.elasticbeanstalk.com/

  - We should now see a sample NodeJS application on this page.

* Our code has 2 parts, the backend and frontend.

  - We put the backend on the Internet FIRST.

  - Then we put the frontend.

* In our "Ecommerce-project-react-env" application, click the "Upload and deploy" button to upload our backend first.

  - We need to upload our backend as a ZIP FILE.

  - Go to VSCode and wee already have a cmd setup to do this.

  - Open terminal and cd into "ecommerce-backend"

  - Run command "npm run zip".

  - This cmd will save all the backend code into a zip file.

  - Upload zip file and then click "Deploy"

  - Elastic Beanstalk will now take the backend code and put it on the Internet.

  - Click the domain link again when deployment is done and we should see a different application now.
    - "index.html not found" is expected

    - Go to the url and at the end, add "/api/products"

      - This will give us the products data.
        - http://ecommerce-project-react-env.eba-d3sphc7z.us-east-2.elasticbeanstalk.com/api/products

* We can now upload our FRONTEND code.
  - We also need to prep it.

  - Notice, we use a lot of jsx files in our frontend, and the browser does NOT understand JSX, only normal JavaScript.

  - We need to CONVERT jsx codee to JavaScript. can do so with vite command "npm run build".
    - Converts the code and saves it in a folder called "dist".

    - Inside "dist" we have an:
      - "index.html" file that contains HTML for react website.

      - "assets" folder has a "index-BM...." JS file that contains all our React code converted into JS, all COMBINED into ONE file.
        - It also minified(compressed) the code.

      - "assets" folder has a "index-BM...." CSS file that does the same thing with compression.

      - Frontend is now OPTIMIZED

* One way to download our React code is:
  - Put our frontend code in another service like "Github Pages"
  
  - Then when we visit the website our browser will download the frontend code.

* Another way is to notice, we ALREADY have our BACKEND computer running on Elastic Beanstalk.

  - We can just REUSE this backend computer.

  - Put our React code into the backend computer.

  - Our browser will then download the React code from the BACKEND computer.

  - Common setup that simplifies setup, since we only need to set up ONE computer.

  - In VSCode, take "dist" folder and drag it into our "ecommerce-backend" folder.

  - Go back to terminal, cd into "ecommerce-backend" and recreate the zip file with the "npm run zip" command.

  - Upload this new zip file to Elastic Beanstalk.
    - Click "Upload and deploy"

    - Select new zip file 2.

    - Click "Deploy"

    - When done, click the domain URL again.

* Our React app is now on the Internet!

* How does this work?
  - 1st, we put our backend on the Internet, so it runs on a separate computer.

  - Then, we put our frontend code in the backend computer.

  - We have some code in the backend, where when we visit the backend using the URL path '/", the backend sends back the React code to our computer.

  - We now have the React code running on our computer and it communicates with the backend running on another computer.

* We can confifgure vite to put the "dist" folder into the "ecommerce-backend" folder directly.
  - Go to "vite.config.js"

  - "outDir" property tells vite where to put the "dist" folder. use the path file to cd into the proper folder.

  - build: {
    outDir: '../ecommerce-backend/dist'
  }

  - Delete old "dist" folder from "ecommerce-backend".

  - Run "npm run build" in "ecommerce-project".

  - The "dist" folder should now be in the "ecommerce-backend" folder.

* How do we deploy a React app without a backend?
  - What if we don't know how to write backend code yet???

  - We can actually just put the code on the Internet directly.
    - EX: chatbot-project does not use a backend, only React code.

  - To deploy this project, we can cd into "chatbot-project" and run "npm run build"

  - This converts react code into normal JS and save it in a "dist" folder.

  - If we just have a frontend, we can upload it directly to a service like Github Pages.

* Load Balancer: adds ANOTHER computer in front of our backend computer
  - When we have a really busy website with a lot of traffic, ONE computer may not be enough to handle all the requests.

  - We can run the backend on MULTIPLE computers. The load balancer will DISTRIBUTE the requests across the many computers. Allows us to handle more users.

* Let's optimize our AWS deployment with Load Balancers.
  - Go to "Environment" tab and then click on "Configuration" tab.

  - Scroll down to "Instance traffic and scaling" section.

  - Click "Edit" and then scroll to "Capacity" section.

  - Go to "Environment Type" dropdown and select "Load Balanced".

  - This adds ANOTHER computer in front of our backend computer, called a Load Balancer.

  - We can use "Instances" to decide on how many backend computers to run. Min and max.

  - Scroll down and click "Apply"

  - Elastic Beanstalk will now setup a Load Balancer computer in front of our backend computer.

* Currently all the data for our React app(products, cart, etc) are stored in a database.
  - Saved in a sqlite file.

  - When we put the React app on the Intenret, we usually put the database on a SEPARATE COMPUTER.

  - This lets us share the data between backend computers.

* Save data onto separate computer.
  - Go to "Environment" tab and then click on "Configuration" tab.

  - Scroll down to "networking and database" section.

  - Click "Edit" and scroll down to "database"

  - Click "Enable database"

  - Elastic Beanstalk will automatically create a new computer running a database and connect it to the backend.

  - We can select the database we want to use. "Engine", "Engine version", "Instance class", "Storage"

  - "Instance Class" = size of the computer running the database.
    - Select "db.t3.micro"

    - Set a password

  - Scroll down and click "Apply"
  
  - AWS will setup a separate computer to run the database.

* Setup a domain name to make our website easier to access.
  - Purchase a domain name first.

  - Use the "Route 53" service in AWS.

  - Route 53 lets us manage domain names in AWS.

  - Click "Create Hosted Zone"

  - Hosted Zone = lets us connect a domain name to our app.

* Fix the "Not Secure" error by getting an SSL Certificate on AWS.
  - SSL Certificate = special file that enables encryption.

  - Search for "Certificate Manager" service in AWS.

  - Click "Request a certificate", then "request a public certificate"

  - Enter the domain name you created.

  - Make sure the "validation method" "DNS validation" is selected.

